00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
medicines are essential to save lives  and treat people who suffer from  coronavirus our priority is to ensure  that Europeans have access to the  medicines they need during this public  health crisis since the start of the  outbreak we have monitored the situation  with the support of our European Agency  for medicine we have engaged with the  industry to anticipate any possible  disruption in the supply of these  critical products indeed we need larger  quantities of medicines to treat  coronavirus patients and hospitals and  this is why today we adopted guidelines  to ensure that Europeans have access to  affordable medicines during this crisis  we ask that national governments lift  any export ban on medicines such  restrictions can lead to shortages  elsewhere in the European Union and it  is equally important that they avoid  stockpiling because this prevents  medicines from being in the pharmacies  and the hospitals where they are needed  but we also need increased production of  medicines industry needs to reorganize  its supply chains and production lines  accordingly and we help the industry to  do that including by giving them some  flexibility in regulation or  facilitating state aid where possible  and national authorities should consider  limiting the sale on line of medicines  that are essential for treating  coronavirus patients and finally we need  to step up action against disinformation  it can push citizens to stockpile or use  the wrong medicines so we have to  counter it and provide transparent  information to the public about the  available supply of medicines we are  faced with a pandemic and we see it as  our duty even more than usual to help  ensure that Europeans have the medicines  they need
 

